<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bungo Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bungo Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, corresponding to most of modern Ōita Prefecture. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period it was the home base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-clan">Ōtomo clan</a>, whose port town of Funai traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Portuguese in the 16th century. After the Ōtomo lost it in 1593 it was divided among several small domains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bungo-province.jpg" alt="Bungo Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Bungo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bungo Province was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the area of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>, corresponding to most of modern Ōita Prefecture, except what are now the cities of Nakatsu and Usa. It bordered Hyūga to the south, Higo and Chikugo to the west, and Chikuzen and Buzen to the north. In the Gokishichidō system it belonged to the Saikaidō circuit, and under the Engishiki classification it was ranked a superior country in importance and a far country in distance from the capital.</p><p>During the Kofun period the area had three main power centres, on the Kunisaki Peninsula and around what are now Ōita District and Hita District, each ruled by a kuni no miyatsuko. By the Asuka period it had been consolidated into a single Toyo Province, which was divided into Bungo and Buzen after the Taika Reforms and the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ritsuryo">Ritsuryō</a> system in 701. The Bungo no Kuni Fudoki, compiled in the first half of the 8th century, is one of only five fudoki in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> that survive in almost complete form.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1185, after the Heike clan was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-clan">Ōtomo clan</a> as shugo over Bungo to suppress pro-Taira <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>. The Ōtomo ruled the area from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period into the Muromachi period and expanded their control into neighbouring provinces. In the Sengoku period Portuguese traders brought firearms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and the Funai area became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> activity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-sorin">Ōtomo Sōrin</a> met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> in 1551 and later converted. The Jesuit records call him the King of Bungo. He sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> in the 1550s and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tensho-embassy">Tenshō embassy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1582, and he forced his subjects to convert and ordered the destruction of Buddhist temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> shrines.</p><p>In 1578 Sōrin came into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a> to the south and, after a series of defeats, turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> for help. After Hideyoshi's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> in 1587, Sōrin's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-yoshimune">Ōtomo Yoshimune</a> was restored to control of Bungo. Following Hideyoshi's expulsion of missionaries he recanted his baptism and began a campaign to exterminate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the province. He was later accused of cowardice during the Japanese invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and was dispossessed, and Bungo was divided into small holdings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the Edo period Bungo was not dominated by a single daimyō. It was divided between territory governed directly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> and a number of small feudal domains. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> each domain briefly became a prefecture, and on 25 December 1871 they were merged to form Ōita Prefecture. A government assessment of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a> counted 1,812 villages in the province, with a total kokudaka of 466,611 koku.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bungo Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Otomo had held <b>Bungo</b> in northeastern Kyushu as military governors since the Kamakura period.</blockquote><a href="/state/otomo-clan-domain">Otomo clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
